- **Release-commit trust boundary**: `release-candidate` GitHub Actions job requires a commit to be BOTH (a) associated with a merged PR whose branch matches `callisto/version-packages` or `callisto/version-packages--<40hex>`, AND (b) GitHub-verified/signed (`commit.verification.verified == true`). Only squash-merge and merge-commit strategies produce GitHub's own signature (`committer: "GitHub"`); rebase-merge does not (`committer: <the human>`, `verified: false`).
- **GITHUB_TOKEN restriction on public repos**: cannot write `.github/workflows/*` via git push OR via GraphQL `createCommitOnBranch`'s `fileChanges`, on any ref. A ref MOVE (not a content write) is not subject to this restriction — this is the basis of the forge-commit-API executor redesign (PR #49).
- **"Persist and verify" vs "recompute and hope it matches"**: the core architectural insight of the current work. `ReleaseDecisionV1` (in `callisto-model/src/release.rs`) already has a `digest: DecisionDigest` field computed via `decision_digest()`/`CanonicalTranscript::decision_v1()`, and its custom `Deserialize` impl already re-validates entries and rejects any decision whose digest doesn't match — a ready-made tamper-detection primitive that was never wired into the automated release-PR flow.
