      "name": "artifact_name_for_package_platform",
      "signature": "pub(crate) fn artifact_name_for_package_platform(package_name: &str, platform: &str, arch: &str, abi: Option<&str>) -> String",
      "description": "Replaces `artifact_name_for_triple(triple: &str) -> String`. First sanitizes `package_name` by replacing every `/` with `-` (actions/upload-artifact forbids `/` in artifact names, and an npm scoped package name such as `@scope/addon` legitimately contains one), then returns `format!(\"{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}\")` with the sanitized name when `abi` is `None`, or `format!(\"{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}-{abi}\")` when `abi` is `Some`, matching napi-rs's own published-platform-package and recommended-CI-artifact-name convention (`<name>-<platform>-<arch>[-<abi>]`). `build_platform_target` (crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs:138-155), which already receives `package_name` as a parameter, calls `triple_to_role(triple)` (crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs) to destructure `(platform, arch, abi)` and passes those values -- not the raw triple string -- into this function when constructing `PlatformTarget.artifact_name`."
    },
    {
      "name": "callisto-action native artifact placement loop",
      "signature": "New shell logic inside `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s existing `callisto-orchestrate` composite step, placed inside the `[[ -n \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" && \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" != \"false\" ]]` guard (action.yml:167) immediately after `NATIVE_MATRIX` is computed (action.yml:164-165) and before `callisto plan-publish`/publish executes.",
      "description": "Feeds `NATIVE_MATRIX` through process substitution, not a pipe -- `while IFS= read -r ENTRY; do ... done < <(printf '%s' \"$NATIVE_MATRIX\" | jq -c '.[]')` -- matching the existing pattern used for the releases loop at action.yml:219, so an `exit 1` inside the loop body terminates the step itself, not a subshell. For each ENTRY: extracts `ARTIFACT=$(echo \"$ENTRY\" | jq -r '.artifactName')` and `DIR_RAW=$(echo \"$ENTRY\" | jq -r '.packageDir')`; sets `DIR` to `.` when `DIR_RAW` is the empty string (the real value `packageDir` takes for a workspace-root package, per crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/matrix.rs:34-39), otherwise `DIR=$DIR_RAW`; runs `mkdir -p \"$DIR\"` explicitly; then runs `gh run download \"$GITHUB_RUN_ID\" --name \"$ARTIFACT\" --dir \"$DIR\" || { echo \"::error::failed to download artifact '$ARTIFACT' for placement into '$DIR' within run $GITHUB_RUN_ID\"; exit 1; }`. Runs once per array element, in array order. Skipped entirely (loop body never executes) when `NATIVE_MATRIX` is `[]`."
    }
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Given two distinct packages in the same workspace that each declare the same platform target triple (e.g. both declare aarch64-apple-darwin), `callisto matrix --format json`'s output contains two distinct entries for that triple -- one per package -- and their `artifactName` values differ, each shaped `<sanitized packageName>-<platform>-<arch>` (or `<sanitized packageName>-<platform>-<arch>-<abi>` when the triple carries an abi, e.g. a musl or gnu linux triple), where `<sanitized packageName>` is that entry's own packageName with every `/` character replaced with `-`, and `<platform>`/`<arch>`/`<abi>` are the same platform/arch/abi values that entry's own `platform`/`arch`/`abi` fields report. For example, packages `pkg-a` and `pkg-b` both declaring `aarch64-apple-darwin` produce `artifactName` values `pkg-a-darwin-arm64` and `pkg-b-darwin-arm64` -- distinct because packageName differs, containing no raw triple substring (e.g. no `aarch64-apple-darwin` appearing anywhere in the result), no vendor component (e.g. `apple`, `pc`, `unknown`), and no fixed `native-` prefix. Separately, a package named `@scope/addon` declaring `aarch64-apple-darwin` produces `artifactName` `@scope-addon-darwin-arm64` -- the packageName's `/` replaced with `-` -- containing no `/` anywhere in the result.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Given the same two-packages-one-triple scenario as AC-001, `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s `NATIVE_MATRIX` computation (which pipes callisto matrix's output through `unique_by(.artifactName)`) retains both packages' entries rather than collapsing them to one, because their artifactName values are now distinct.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Given a workflow run where N per-platform build jobs each upload an artifact via actions/upload-artifact using the exact artifactName string from that platform's callisto matrix --format json entry, and the callisto-orchestrate step's placement loop (placed inside the `[[ -n \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" && \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" != \"false\" ]]` guard, not merely somewhere after NATIVE_MATRIX computation) executes: after the step completes, each of the N artifacts' downloaded file contents are present under the corresponding packageDir path relative to the working directory (with the directory having been created via the loop's own mkdir -p if it did not already exist), and this placement completes before the step's callisto plan-publish/publish invocation begins.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "Given nativeMatrix is [] (no native platform-target packages in the workspace) and INPUT_PUBLISH is non-empty and not the literal string \"false\" (so the guard at action.yml:167 is entered), the placement loop body never executes (zero gh run download invocations) because NATIVE_MATRIX's parsed array is empty, and the step proceeds directly to callisto plan-publish/publish exactly as it did before this change. Given INPUT_PUBLISH is unset or the literal string \"false\", the placement loop is never reached regardless of nativeMatrix's contents, because the loop is placed inside that same guard.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Given a nativeMatrix entry whose packageDir field is the empty string (the value produced for a workspace-root package whose manifest has no parent directory component), the placement loop sets DIR to \".\" before calling mkdir -p and gh run download --dir, and the corresponding artifact's contents are placed directly under the working directory rather than the step failing or attempting mkdir -p with an empty path.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Given a nativeMatrix entry whose artifactName was never uploaded within the current workflow run ($GITHUB_RUN_ID) -- including the case where an artifact with that exact name exists in a different, older, or unrelated workflow run in the same repository -- the loop's `gh run download \"$GITHUB_RUN_ID\" --name \"$ARTIFACT\" --dir \"$DIR\"` invocation exits non-zero because the run-id pins the lookup to the current run only, the `|| { ...; exit 1; }` clause terminates the step (not merely a pipeline subshell) before reaching callisto plan-publish/publish, and the step's log output names the specific missing artifactName and the DIR value (post `.`-normalization where applicable) that download attempt was targeting.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md \u00a712's example workflow code block (the fenced code block following the \"Zero-Config GitHub Actions Workflow Pattern\" heading) contains no occurrence of the string \"callisto publish-target\" anywhere within it; the status note above the heading (naming Java/JNI, .NET native-AOT, and the callisto publish-target subcommand as unimplemented) is unchanged and still present.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md \u00a712's example workflow's build job uses the namespaced GitHub Actions matrix form matching callisto-matrix.md \u00a76.1's own documented consumption pattern: `strategy.matrix.target` is set to `${{ fromJson(needs.matrix.outputs.nativeMatrix) }}`, and every per-entry field is dereferenced under the `matrix.target.` prefix -- `runs-on: ${{ matrix.target.hostRunner }}`, plus `matrix.target.triple`, `matrix.target.artifactName`, and `matrix.target.packageDir` in the build/upload steps -- with each of these four resolving to a real field on PlatformTarget's camelCase JSON serialization (hostRunner, triple, artifactName, packageDir). The block contains no bare, non-namespaced `matrix.os` or `matrix.package` reference (neither is a real field under any prefix), and contains no use of `matrix.target` as a plain scalar value (e.g. `${{ matrix.target }}` alone) -- `matrix.target` appears only as the namespace prefix for a specific field.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-009",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md \u00a712's example workflow code block contains all of: (a) a build job's `actions/upload-artifact` step with `name: ${{ matrix.target.artifactName }}`; (b) a release job step invoking `./.github/actions/callisto-action` with `publish: true`; (c) a comment block immediately preceding that callisto-action step containing both the substring \"uploaded artifact into its packageDir\" and the substring \"automatically inside this step\" -- documenting that placement happens automatically within the action, not via a separate consumer-authored step. The block contains no consumer-authored `gh run download` invocation or other literal placement step, consistent with AC-010 and AC-011.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-010",
      "criterion": "A consumer workflow that already uses callisto-action for publishing and adds only a matrix-driven build job (per .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md \u00a76.1's nativeMatrix consumption pattern) uploading artifacts named per artifactName, with no additional bash, composite action, or script authored by the consumer to perform placement, ends with the callisto-orchestrate step having invoked gh run download once per nativeMatrix entry and each entry's artifact contents present at that entry's packageDir (post `.`-normalization) -- with no requirement on this spec's part that callisto publish itself subsequently succeeds, per this spec's non-goal on the publish-side packaging round-trip.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-011",
      "criterion": ".claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md \u00a76.2's standalone reference snippet (for consumers building their own custom multi-job pipeline outside callisto-action, where nativeMatrix genuinely crosses a `needs.<job>.outputs.nativeMatrix` job-output boundary) is updated to pin the download to `$GITHUB_RUN_ID` and to `mkdir -p \"$DIR\"` before calling `gh run download`, and \u00a76.2 gains an explicit note stating that consumers using callisto-action for publishing do not need to write this snippet themselves -- callisto-action performs equivalent placement automatically, in-step, once nativeMatrix is non-empty and publish is enabled.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-012",
      "criterion": ".claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md \u00a74 (\"Artifact naming convention\") contains no occurrence of the unsanitized string `@scope/addon-darwin-arm64` anywhere in the section; the section's illustration of the napi-rs convention instead shows the sanitized form `@scope-addon-darwin-arm64`, and the section contains a sentence stating that `packageName` has every `/` character replaced with `-` before formatting.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-001",
  "revision_note": "Three corrections across two passes. (1) AC-008, corrected per lambda:exit-gate's contradiction report and already verified accurate by canon:gate-spec -- unchanged in this round. Original AC-008 required ARCHITECTURE.md \u00a712's example to use a bare `${{ matrix.hostRunner }}` form and forbade any `matrix.target` reference. Direct inspection of the committed ARCHITECTURE.md (lines 486-501) shows the implemented example correctly uses `strategy: matrix: target: ${{ fromJson(...) }}` with namespaced `matrix.target.<field>` dereferences (hostRunner, triple, artifactName, packageDir) -- the only valid GitHub Actions form for that strategy shape, and the exact pattern already published as canonical in .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md \u00a76.1 lines 287-289 (the same pattern AC-010 itself cites). AC-008 was rewritten to require the namespaced `matrix.target.<field>` form and forbid only the genuinely nonexistent bare `matrix.os`/`matrix.package` fields and bare-scalar `matrix.target` use. (2) AC-009, corrected per canon:gate-spec's FAIL blocker on this AC-008-corrected spec. Original AC-009 required the example to, 'read end to end, show... a placement step downloading that artifact into its package directory' -- an untestable human-reading judgment with no mechanical predicate (unlike sibling AC-007/AC-008's greppable string conditions), and one that contradicted AC-010 (no consumer-authored placement script) and AC-011 (callisto-action performs placement automatically in-step): a literal reading of AC-009 would require adding a `gh run download` step to \u00a712's example, violating the pattern AC-010/AC-011 establish. Direct inspection of ARCHITECTURE.md lines 474-513 confirms the example represents placement only via a three-line comment (lines 507-509: 'Placement of each build job's uploaded artifact into its packageDir, / then callisto publish, both happen automatically inside this step / once nativeMatrix is non-empty and publish is enabled.') immediately preceding the `./.github/actions/callisto-action` step with `publish: true`; there is no literal placement step anywhere in the block. AC-009 was rewritten to require exactly this observed structure -- the `actions/upload-artifact` step's `name: ${{ matrix.target.artifactName }}`, the callisto-action invocation with `publish: true`, and a preceding comment containing the two exact observed substrings -- and to explicitly forbid any consumer-authored `gh run download` step, making AC-009 consistent with AC-010 and AC-011 rather than contradicting them. Checked all other criteria for dependency on either claim: none reference matrix field forms or the placement-step-presence question, so no further criteria required revision. AC-008 and AC-009 left byte-identical in this round per instruction. (3) AC-001/scope/api_surface/non_goals[17], corrected in a prior round after direct inspection of the committed crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs (lines 36-52, 138-155) contradicted the spec's prior claim of an `artifact_name_for_package_triple(package_name, triple) -> \"native-{package_name}-{triple}\"` function. The function actually implemented and tested (see `artifact_name_for_package_platform_embeds_package_name_and_platform_arch_abi` and `build_platform_target_combines_role_and_ci_table` in matrix.rs's own test module) is `artifact_name_for_package_platform(package_name: &str, platform: &str, arch: &str, abi: Option<&str>) -> String`, returning `{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}` (or with `-{abi}` appended when `abi` is `Some`) -- e.g. `native-mod-darwin-arm64`, `other-pkg-linux-x64-musl`. `build_platform_target` calls `triple_to_role` (crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs:136-163, proven bijective across all 18 known triples by the existing `triple_to_role_known_triples_round_trip` test) to destructure the triple into `(platform, arch, abi)` before calling the naming function, rather than passing the raw triple through. This is napi-rs's own documented published-platform-package and recommended-CI-artifact-name convention (https://napi.rs/docs/deep-dive/release: `<name>-<platform>-<arch>[-<abi>]`, e.g. `@scope/addon-darwin-arm64`, `@scope/addon-win32-x64-msvc`) rather than the raw-triple-with-vendor-component convention (e.g. cargo-dist's `ripgrep-15.2.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz`), which belongs to a different, already-excluded (Tier 2 / GitHub Release asset) distribution model. scope's Rust-change sentence, the api_surface entry (renamed from `artifact_name_for_package_triple` to `artifact_name_for_package_platform` with the real signature), AC-001 (rewritten with a concrete falsifiable example matching the real format), and non_goals[17] (reworded to name both stale formats it supersedes -- the original raw-triple format and the never-implemented triple-shaped intermediate format -- while keeping the 'artifactName is an opaque CI-internal correlation token, not a versioned contract' point and the no-config-override rationale) were corrected accordingly. AC-002 through AC-011 (excluding AC-008/AC-009, untouched per instruction) were checked for references to the stale function name or triple-shaped format; none contain any -- AC-002's `unique_by(.artifactName)` dedup logic is format-agnostic and holds unchanged under the corrected format, so no further criteria required revision. (4) AC-001/scope/api_surface/non_goals, corrected in this round after a further contradiction report showed that even the prior round's 'corrected' description of `artifact_name_for_package_platform` was itself incomplete: direct re-inspection of the live crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs (lines 36-52) shows the function sanitizes `package_name` -- replacing every `/` with `-` -- before formatting, a step the spec had never described. This matters because a PackageId's name can legitimately be an npm scoped name (e.g. `@scope/addon`), and `actions/upload-artifact` rejects `/` in artifact names; embedding `@scope/addon` verbatim would have produced the invalid artifactName `@scope/addon-darwin-arm64`, breaking every `actions/upload-artifact`/`gh run download` call for that package. Confirmed against the function's own test `artifact_name_for_package_platform_sanitizes_scoped_npm_package_name` (matrix.rs:787-791), which asserts `artifact_name_for_package_platform(\"@scope/addon\", \"darwin\", \"arm64\", None) == \"@scope-addon-darwin-arm64\"` and that the result contains no `/`. AC-001's criterion text was extended with this exact verified `@scope/addon` -> `@scope-addon-darwin-arm64` example layered onto the existing general `<sanitized packageName>-<platform>-<arch>[-<abi>]` shape and the existing pkg-a/pkg-b distinctness example (both kept, since both remain valid). The api_surface entry's description was updated to state the sanitization step and its rationale. scope's Rust-change sentence was updated to describe the sanitization step in the same place it already described the function's return value. Checked AC-002 through AC-011 for dependency on the unsanitized claim: none reference the exact artifactName string format except AC-001 itself, so none required further revision; AC-002's dedup guarantee remains format-agnostic. Sanitization does introduce one narrow theoretical edge case AC-001/AC-002's distinctness guarantee does not additionally cover -- two packageName values differing only in whether a literal `/` or `-` occupies the same position (e.g. `a/b` vs. a separately-named `a-b` in the same workspace declaring the same triple) would sanitize to an identical artifactName and collide. This was not left unaddressed: a new non_goals entry was added stating this explicitly is not guarded against, with rationale (npm scoped names use `/` exactly once as the mandatory `@scope/name` separator; Cargo and PyPI names disallow `/` entirely; artifactName is already documented as an opaque correlation token, not a namespace independently guaranteed unique from packageName choice) for why it is negligible rather than a defect requiring a new acceptance criterion.",
  "reasoning": "Verified directly against live source: crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs's artifact_name_for_package_platform (lines 36-52) sanitizes package_name via .replace('/', \"-\") before formatting, and the test artifact_name_for_package_platform_sanitizes_scoped_npm_package_name (line 787) confirms artifact_name_for_package_platform(\"@scope/addon\", \"darwin\", \"arm64\", None) == \"@scope-addon-darwin-arm64\" with no '/' in the result. AC-001, api_surface[0].description, and scope's Rust-change sentence were rewritten to state the sanitization rule. Checked AC-002 through AC-011 individually: none quote or depend on the exact artifactName string shape besides AC-001; AC-002 depends only on distinctness surviving through unique_by(.artifactName), which sanitization does not undermine in the general case. Chose non_goals over a new acceptance criterion for the residual a/b-vs-a-b collision because guarding against it would require either rejecting '/'-containing non-scoped names outright (a breaking behavior change out of this spec's scope) or a more elaborate collision-detection mechanism neither requested nor evidenced as needed by any real workspace; the existing artifactName-is-an-opaque-token framing already carried in non_goals covers the same reasoning, so this is additive to an established pattern rather than a new prec

